```
Reason:
In the `transfer_one` function, when a receive buffer exists (`t->rx_buf` is non-NULL), the code unconditionally calls `mchp_coreqspi_write_read_op`. This function transmits data (garbage when `tx_buf` is NULL) on the multi-bit bus lines simultaneously with reading, which corrupts the transfer in dual/quad read-only mode because the controller generates clock cycles internally and does not expect the driver to drive the lines.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+	bool dual_quad = false;
+	if (t->tx_nbits == SPI_NBITS_QUAD || t->rx_nbits == SPI_NBITS_QUAD ||
+			t->tx_nbits == SPI_NBITS_DUAL ||
+			t->rx_nbits == SPI_NBITS_DUAL)
+		dual_quad = true;
...
	} else if (!dual_quad) {
		qspi->rxbuf = (u8 *)t->rx_buf;
		qspi->rx_len = t->len;
		mchp_coreqspi_write_read_op(qspi);
+	} else {
+		qspi->rxbuf = (u8 *)t->rx_buf;
+		qspi->rx_len = t->len;
+		mchp_coreqspi_read_op(qspi);
 	}
```
Reason:
The fix first detects whether the transfer uses dual or quad bus widths by checking `tx_nbits` and `rx_nbits`. For a read-only transfer in dual/quad mode (`dual_quad` is true and `rx_buf` is non-NULL), the driver now calls `mchp_coreqspi_read_op` instead of `mchp_coreqspi_write_read_op`. This properly allows the hardware to generate the clock cycles and read data without sending any garbage on the multi-bit lines, preventing corruption.

